## MUST

**A — 7(k) and 7(b) contradict each other: the containment helper's read error
carries an absolute path.** Brief `:743-745` (7(k)): "every message produced
across rows (b)–(j) is ASCII, at most 200 characters, contains no `/` outside
the `ci/shots/<id>` form". Brief `:327-328` (ladder step 4) and `:301-302`
(step 1) both interpolate the helper's own error string:
`served-e2e screenshot <name> is unsafe or unreadable: <error>`.
Measured against the real `readContainedRegularFile` (`probe-err.mts`):

```
missing PNG (behaviour 7b):
  code="ENOENT"
  error="ENOENT: no such file or directory, lstat '/tmp/claude-1000/w110-probe-vO4zwO/ci/shots/W-110/board.png'"
  len(wrapped)=159
```

So 7(b) — the Genuine red for behaviour 7, a deleted PNG — produces a finding
carrying an absolute path and six `/` outside the `ci/shots/<id>` form. 7(b)
and 7(k) cannot both pass. The same string also breaks the Interfaces claim at
`:343-345` ("no screenshot byte, no absolute path and no raw manifest text
ever reaches a finding"). And the 200-character bound goes too: 159 characters
in `/tmp/claude-1000/…`; at the brief's own 22-character caps a real
`mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), …))` path plus a 22-character id and a
22-character name reaches roughly 209.

Fix: in steps 1 and 4, switch on the helper's `code` rather than
interpolating its message — `ENOENT` → a fixed "is missing" wording — and pass
through only the helper's own regex-bound refusals, which are clean (measured:
the symlink case returns `code: undefined`,
`error: "path reaches its target through a symlink"`, and the new `maxBytes`
refusal is `target exceeds <n> bytes`). Then 7(k) holds as written.

*Class, with the search.* `is unsafe or unreadable: ${…error}` is already the
house pattern: `packages/commands/src/opus-model.ts:583, 598, 621, 637, 675,
696, 727, 890, 939` — nine existing sites, all of which can emit an absolute
path today, including `validateServed`'s own gate-log read at `:675`. W-110
owns only the two it adds; take those, and file the other nine as a follow-on
rather than widening. The brief's step-1 note ("exactly as the existing log
path does", `:303-304`) leans on that precedent — worth saying that the
precedent is itself leaky, so the two new sites are a deliberate improvement
on it rather than a deviation.

**B — 3(b)'s new derivation reads a field whose value set is not the column
set, so the row cannot pass today.** Brief `:598-601`: "Board renders a column
for **every phase the served lifecycle declares**: the spec reads
`GET /api/officina`'s `lifecycle.states`, takes the set of `phase` values, and
asserts a `[data-column-id]` exists for each." The served shape is right —
`apps/server/src/store.ts:341` does return
`lifecycle: { states: { id, name, phase }[] }` — but the two sets differ in
**both** directions, and `apps/web/src/lib/board.ts:9-13` says so in a comment:

```
export const NEEDS_YOU_COLUMN = "needs_you";
/** Board policy, not the lifecycle's: the `backlog` phase is not a column. Its
 *  opera are cards in Planned, after the greenlit ones (W-129, D-034). Everything
 *  else about the column set comes from the served lifecycle. */
const BACKLOG_PHASE = "backlog";
```

`adapters/native/src/index.ts:40` declares
`{ id: "backlog", name: "Backlog", phase: "backlog" }`, so `backlog` **is** in
`lifecycle.states`' phase set and has **no** `[data-column-id]`; and
`needs_you` is a column with **no** phase behind it. The row is listed as
"passes today" and will fail today, which Acceptance `:803-804` makes a
builder stop ("if a row the brief says passes today fails, the brief is wrong
for it and the builder stops"). Behaviour 3 dies before reaching its Genuine
red.

Revision 1 was right here and the S8 fix overshot: the honest derivation is
`[NEEDS_YOU_COLUMN, ...phases.filter((p) => p !== BACKLOG_PHASE)]`, which
needs `board.ts`'s own two constants — a legitimate import, but then say so,
because it makes the row depend on a module the brief puts out of scope
(`:375` lists `board.ts` among the un-owned files). Otherwise keep revision
1's measured seven literal ids; S8 asked for the coupling to be *stated*, not
for a derivation that does not exist in the data. The row's second clause (the
card-id set equals `/api/opera`'s id set) is unaffected and correct — a
`backlog` opus still gets a card, in Planned.

**C — two rows still miscount against the ladder.** Brief `:741-743` (7(j)):
a `manifest.txt` of 8 KiB + 1 "is refused by the helper's cap at step 1 with
**exactly one** finding". Step 1 (`:301-304`) routes any non-"no such file"
read error to `served-e2e screenshot manifest is unsafe or unreadable:
<error>`, and the brief itself says that message "matching
`/unsafe|unreadable|symlink|regular file/i`, `addPolicyProblem` also raises as
`opus.reference`" — confirmed at `packages/commands/src/opus-model.ts:784-787`.
So it is **two** findings, which is exactly what 7(d) correctly states for its
own symlink case. 7(i)'s over-cap PNG has the same shape: "exactly one `is
unsafe or unreadable:` carrying the helper's 'exceeds' wording" is one
*message* under two *rules*.

Four rows now use three different conventions: 7(b) counts
`opus.ui.e2e` only, 7(d) counts both rules, 7(i) counts messages, 7(j) counts
"findings". Pick one and say it once at the head of behaviour 7, the way the
ladder is now stated once at `:293-295`. This is the residue of round-1 M1 —
reduced from three instances to two, and purely a counting-convention problem
rather than a ladder problem.

*I re-derived every other count against the ladder and they are right:* 7(c)'s
five name mutations each give exactly one `has a malformed entry`, because
sub-order (i) before (ii) catches the loose/strict mismatch first and stops —
and I checked all five mutations (`a/b.png`, `board..png`, `Board.png`,
`.board.png`, `board.jpg`) genuinely fail the strict regex while the loose
`^shot: ` counter still counts them. 7(h)'s three sub-cases land at (ii),
(ii) and (iv) respectively, one each, with no entry read. 7(i)'s 65-entry case
stops at (iii) before any read, and the "all 65 files absent" negative control
is a good way to prove the ceiling fired. 6(c)'s `carries no screenshot
manifest` and 6(f)'s `names another opus's directory` and 6(g)'s id-limit
message carry no `unsafe|unreadable|symlink|regular file` substring, so each is
exactly one finding as asserted. 6(g) is testable: `packages/cli/src/rules/ids.ts`
has only `opus.id.duplicate` and `opus.id.filename`, no length or format rule,
so a 23-character record id loads.

## SHOULD

**D — `validateServed`'s own existing read is left unbounded in the same
function the brief is editing.** `:284-287`: "`validateServed` runs inside
`checkStudio` — on every `check`, `tick`, `verify` and `/api/health` — so it is
the one caller that must not read an unbounded file". Agreed, and that is
exactly why the gate-log read at `opus-model.ts:675`
(`readContainedRegularFile(root, evidence, "ci")`) should also take a cap: it
is in `validateServed`, on the same always-on path, and a 2 GiB `ci/served.log`
is the same hazard the brief just closed for PNGs. Passing a `LOG_MAX_BYTES`
there is one argument in a file the brief already owns, and existing fixtures
are a few hundred bytes. Class enumerated — nine uncapped sites at
`opus-model.ts:583, 598, 621, 637, 675, 696, 727, 890, 939`; take `:675`
(yours), optionally `:696` (`reviewIdentity`, same `checkStudio` path), file the
rest.

**E — step 1 should name the code literal.** `:300`: "A read error whose code
is `no such file`". The helper's `code` comes from the thrown exception's own
`.code`, which is `"ENOENT"` (measured); `"no such file"` is a fragment of the
*message*. And `code` is `undefined` for the helper's own non-fs refusals
(measured on the symlink case), so the branch must tolerate that. Say
`code === "ENOENT"` — the brief is exact about every other regex and constant.

**F — the shots directory is the only `ci/` artifact that is not tree-keyed.**
Every gate log is `<opus>-<probatio>-<tree8>.log`
(`packages/pipeline/src/index.ts:99`), so two verifies on two trees leave two
files and the record's `evidence:` points at the right one.
`ci/shots/<opus>/` is a fixed path that step 2 clears on every run, so a
re-verify or a rebase silently replaces committed evidence and nothing on disk
says which tree the PNGs belong to. Residual 6 (`:922-928`) is otherwise
accurate and I agree with its trust argument in full — add this sentence to
it, because residual 16 has the operator committing the directory.

**G — two stale cross-references.** The compatibility contract's last row
cites `apps/web/dist` consumers at `apps/server/src/http.ts:454`, while the
brief's own measured row at `:95` cites `:510-512`; measured, `serveStatic`'s
`index.html` branch is `http.ts:508-512`. And the `localPipeline` callers row
lists `ci` as a caller — `ci` reaches it only through `runVerify`
(`packages/commands/src/ci.ts:217`); the only `pipeline.run` sites are
`verify.ts:300` and the fallback at `:304`. Both are harmless, but a brief this
precise should not carry a line number that disagrees with its own table.

## Dispositions I verified as properly closed

**M1 — the clause ladder (`:293-341`).** Verified by re-deriving every count
in behaviours 6 and 7 (see C). The ladder is well built: fail-fast per step,
step 4 the one exception, and the sub-order note at `:322-324` names exactly
why (i) must precede (ii). The statement that the gate log's existing clauses
are evaluated before step 1 and are not short-circuited by it (`:339-341`),
pinned by 6(b), closes the question of whether the new ladder could mask the
old checks. Taken.

**M2 — the bounds (`:280-291`).** `maxBytes` is checked against the `fstat`
the helper already performs on the open fd — confirmed, `opus-model.ts:228` is
`const opened = fstatSync(fd);`, and it sits after the `O_NOFOLLOW` open and
before `readFileSync(fd)`, so "post-open, so it cannot be raced" is right.
Additive and optional, so the other nine callers are untouched.
`SHOT_MAX_ENTRIES` 64 × `SHOT_MAX_BYTES` 4 MiB bounds peak memory at 4 MiB
(the Buffer is dropped per iteration) and total I/O at 256 MiB per ui record.
The 8 KiB manifest cap cannot be exceeded by 64 legal entries (~2.6 KiB at the
22-character caps). 7(i) and 7(j) fire both bounds, and 7(i)'s negative
control distinguishes "the ceiling fired" from "65 unreadable entries". Taken.

**M3 — the manifest becomes a file (`:207-269`, `:245-258`).** The coordinator
asked whether the file-based manifest keeps the binding my security pass
wanted. It does, and I checked rather than assumed:

- The reasoning at `:248-249` now states the pipeline mechanism correctly
  (`${r.stdout}${r.stderr}`, stderr last, then tailed) — matching my round-1
  measurement, and the dependency is deleted rather than restated, which is
  what I asked for.
- The per-run cleared directory is real: step 2 `rmSync` + `mkdirSync`
  (`:219-221`), and 5(d) pins it by planting both a `stale.png` and a
  `manifest.txt` and requiring both gone. 5(e) and 5(j) make a green walk that
  produced nothing, or produced an illegal name, write no manifest at all —
  so the script cannot leave a half-truth behind.
- The gate record side is unchanged and still validated: `status: passed`, a
  `tree:` certificate, an exact `at`, and a log corroborating `certifies:`,
  `command: node scripts/served-e2e.mjs` and `exit code: 0`
  (`opus-model.ts:660-684`).
- **No binding was lost.** Revision 1's manifest-in-log was equally forgeable:
  `validateServed` only requires the log to *contain* those three lines, all
  of which a hand-written log can carry. Residual 6 (`:922-928`) says exactly
  this, names the cost of closing it (a third environment variable carrying
  the certificate into the script), and does not overclaim. That is the right
  trade and the right way to record it. My only addition is F above.
- The stdout echo is explicitly non-load-bearing (`:257-258`, residual 9), and
  the Acceptance bullet now feeds the **file** to the rule, not stdout
  (`:813-818`), and runs the whole path through `localPipeline`. That is the
  end-to-end check the design needs.

**S3 — `commandEnv` scoping (`:153-205`). No leak found.** `base =
filterEnv(process.env)` stays outside the loop and the per-command spread is
`extra === undefined ? base : { ...base, ...extra }`, so a gate not named in
`commandEnv` gets the identical object it gets today. I traced every path a
leak could take: `pipeline.run` has exactly two call sites, `verify.ts:300`
and the catch-fallback at `:304`, and **both pass the same `runOpts`** — so the
fallback neither loses the entry nor widens it. `bisellium ci` does not call
the pipeline; it calls `runVerify` in process (`ci.ts:217`). There is no second
live pipeline to leak through: `selectPipeline()` unconditionally returns
`localPipeline` and `noMistakesPipeline.run` unconditionally throws
(`packages/pipeline/src/index.ts:138-151`), so the "an implementation that
ignores `commandEnv`" row is moot in practice. `SERVED_E2E_PROBATIO`'s claimed
home checks out: the `"served-e2e"` literal is at `opus-model.ts:298`, inside
`effectiveProbationes`, so the const belongs there and `@bisellium/pipeline`
still learns no gate id. 4(c) and 4(d) pin both halves — scoped to the one id,
and `tests` still sees today's env. Taken.

